{ type: install
  message: <<EOM
Container networking relies on NAT to allow container network packets
out to the host's network. This requires a PF firewall to perform the
translation. 5BSD runs OpenBSD PF; a ruleset for it is included:

# cp /usr/local/etc/containers/pf.conf.sample /etc/pf.conf
# sysrc pf_enable=YES
# service pf start

The sample translates on the "egress" interface group, which the kernel
keeps pointing at the interface(s) of the default routes, so it needs no
editing for a host with one uplink. NAT is a 'match out ... nat-to' rule;
it takes effect for packets a pass rule lets through.

Port redirections (podman run -p) are loaded by the portmap plugin into
anchors nested under cni-rdr: 'pass in ... rdr-to' for other hosts and
'pass out on lo0 ... rdr-to ... nat-to' for connections from the container
host itself, including localhost. The main ruleset must contain
'anchor "cni-rdr/*"'; OpenBSD PF has no rdr-anchor or nat-anchor. No
sysctl is needed.

pf.ko, if_bridge and if_epair must be loaded at boot: 5BSD denies late
module loads. 5BSD's loader preloads them by default.

If upgrading from an older version, replace any rdr-anchor/nat-anchor lines
and FreeBSD "nat on ... ->" rules in /etc/pf.conf as in the sample.

For example if host port 1234 is redirected to an http service running in a
container, you could connect to it using:

# fetch -o- http://$(hostname):1234

or

# fetch -o- http://localhost:1234
EOM
}
